## TypeScript User Type Declaration

### Interface Declaration

```typescript
/**
 * Represents a user in the system.
 */
interface User {
  /** Unique identifier for the user */
  readonly id: string;

  /** Full display name of the user */
  name: string;

  /** User's email address */
  email: string;

  /** Optional URL pointing to the user's avatar image */
  avatarUrl?: string;
}
```

---

### Alternative Approaches

#### Using a `type` alias

```typescript
type User = {
  readonly id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
};
```

#### With stricter email and URL branding

```typescript
type Email = string & { readonly _brand: "Email" };
type AvatarUrl = string & { readonly _brand: "AvatarUrl" };

interface User {
  readonly id: string;
  name: string;
  email: Email;
  avatarUrl?: AvatarUrl;
}
```

#### With a utility type for partial updates

```typescript
interface User {
  readonly id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  avatarUrl?: string;
}

/** Used when creating a new user (id is auto-generated) */
type CreateUserPayload = Omit<User, "id">;

/** Used when updating an existing user (all fields optional except id) */
type UpdateUserPayload = Pick<User, "id"> & Partial<Omit<User, "id">>;
```

---

### Usage Examples

```typescript
// Valid user object
const user: User = {
  id: "usr_123",
  name: "Jane Doe",
  email: "jane@example.com",
  avatarUrl: "https://example.com/avatars/jane.png",
};

// Valid user without avatar
const userWithoutAvatar: User = {
  id: "usr_456",
  name: "John Smith",
  email: "john@example.com",
};

// Create payload (no id required)
const newUser: CreateUserPayload = {
  name: "Alice",
  email: "alice@example.com",
};

// Update payload (only id is required)
const update: UpdateUserPayload = {
  id: "usr_123",
  name: "Jane Smith",
};
```

---

### Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Reason |
|---|---|
| `readonly id` | Prevents accidental mutation of the primary key |
| `avatarUrl?: string` | Optional field — not all users have an avatar |
| `interface` over `type` | Preferred for object shapes; supports declaration merging |
| Branded types (optional) | Adds compile-time safety for email/URL format validation |

## TypeScript User Type Declaration

### Interface Declaration

```typescript
