## Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **`PayloadValue` recursive type** | Allows arbitrary nested JSON without `any` |
| **`EventParseError` with `field`** | Callers can pinpoint exactly which field failed |
| **Accepts `string \| number \| Date` for timestamp** | Handles ISO strings from JSON and epoch numbers from APIs |
| **`undefined`/`null` payload → `{}`** | Payload is optional in practice; avoids forcing callers to include it |
| **Defensive `new Date(raw)`** | Prevents mutation if the caller later modifies their Date object |
| **No third-party deps** | Zero-dependency; swap in Zod/Valibot if schema complexity grows |

## TypeScript Fetch Wrapper

### Type Declaration

```typescript
interface RequestOptions {
  method: "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "PATCH" | "DELETE" | "HEAD" | "OPTIONS";
  url: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  body?: BodyInit | null;
}
```

### Implementation

```typescript
async function fetchRequest(options: RequestOptions): Promise<Response> {
  const { method, url, headers, body } = options;

  if (!url || url.trim() === "") {
    throw new Error("URL must be a non-empty string.");
  }

  const hasBody = !["GET", "HEAD"].includes(method);

  const requestInit: RequestInit = {
    method,
    headers: {
      "Content-Type": "application/json",
      Accept: "application/json",
      ...headers,
    },
    ...(hasBody && body !== undefined ? { body } : {}),
  };

  try {
    const response = await fetch(url, requestInit);

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(
        `Request failed — Status: ${response.status} ${response.statusText} | URL: ${url}`
      );
    }

    return response;
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof TypeError) {
      throw new TypeError(`Network error or invalid URL: ${url}`);
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

### Usage Examples

```typescript
// GET request
const getResponse = await fetchRequest({
  method: "GET",
  url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1",
});
const data = await getResponse.json();
console.log(data);

// POST request with body and custom headers
const postResponse = await fetchRequest({
  method: "POST",
  url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts",
  headers: {
    Authorization: "Bearer my-token",
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: "Hello", body: "World", userId: 1 }),
});
const created = await postResponse.json();
console.log(created);

// DELETE request
const deleteResponse = await fetchRequest({
  method: "DELETE",
  url: "https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1",
});
console.log(deleteResponse.status); // 200
```

---

### Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Reason |
|---|---|
| `method` as a union type | Restricts to valid HTTP methods at compile time |
| `headers` as `Record<string, string>` | Matches the standard `HeadersInit` key-value shape |
| `body` as `BodyInit \| null` | Supports strings, `FormData`, `Blob`, `URLSearchParams`, etc. |
| Body excluded for `GET`/`HEAD` | These methods must not include a body per the HTTP spec |
| Default `Content-Type` header | Sensible default, overridable via `headers` spread |
| `response.ok` check | Converts non-2xx HTTP responses into thrown errors |
| `TypeError` catch | Distinguishes network failures from HTTP-level errors |

## TypeScript Fetch Wrapper

### Type Declaration

```typescript
interface RequestOptions {
  method: "GET" | "POST" | "PUT" | "PATCH" | "DELETE" | "HEAD" | "OPTIONS";
  url: string;
