Of course. Here are a few ways to declare a `User` shape in TypeScript, from the most common to alternatives.

### 1. Using an `interface` (Most Common)

This is the most idiomatic way to define the shape of an object in TypeScript.

```typescript
interface User {
  id: number | string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
  avatar?: string; // The '?' makes this property optional
}

// --- Example Usage ---

// A user with an avatar
const user1: User = {
  id: 1,
  name: 'Jane Doe',
  email: 'jane.doe@example.com',
  avatar: 'https://example.com/avatars/jane.png'
};

// A user without an avatar (this is valid because avatar is optional)
const user2: User = {
  id: 'a1b2-c3d4-e5f6', // ID can also be a string
  name: 'John Smith',
  email: 'john.smith@example.com'
};
```

